course: dictionary
lesson: 8685
title: Seesaw: a word that balances
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A seesaw is a long board balanced in the middle, with a child sitting at each end. When one end goes up, the other goes down. Seesaw can also be a verb: to move up and down, or keep changing between two states.
explain: «A seesaw is a lever with the pivot in the middle, so a child sitting far out can lift a grown-up sitting close in.» The pivot is the point in the middle where the board balances. Sitting further from the middle gives you more pull, so a light child far out can lift a heavy grown-up sitting near the middle.
explain: Context helps you choose the right meaning. On a playground, seesaw is the board. In the news, «seesawing prices» would mean prices keep going up and down, not that anyone is sitting on a board. Ask yourself what would fit here, then check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: A seesaw is a lever with the pivot in the middle.
fact: A seesaw is a long board balanced in the middle, where two children sit at opposite ends and go up and down.
fact: As a verb, «seesaw» means to move up and down, or back and forth, again and again.
fact: In the USA, a seesaw is also called a teeter-totter.
fact: Cranes and lifts use a heavy counterweight, a bit like two children balancing on a seesaw.
exercise: What is a seesaw? | A bridge over a stream | A slide with steps up one side | A tall swing hung from a frame | *A long board balanced in the middle, with a child at each end
exercise: In «seesawing prices», the word seesawing means what? | Getting bigger and never smaller | Breaking into two pieces | *Going up and down, changing again and again | Staying still and never changing
exercise: Why must a heavier child sit nearer the middle? | Because the middle is the warmest part | *So the board can balance with a lighter child sitting further out | So the board is easier to paint | So the heavier child can go higher in the air
test: Which word means the same as teeter-totter, the name used in the USA? | Swing | Slide | *Seesaw | Roundabout
test: In «A seesaw is a lever with the pivot in the middle», what is the middle point called? | *Pivot | Counterweight | Lever | Motor
exam: In a crane, what does the heavy counterweight do, like a seesaw partner? | It makes the load heavier, so the motor works harder | It is only decoration on the arm | *It balances the load, so the motor only has to lift the difference | It holds the crane still so it cannot move
